1. Search Scope

Always limit searches and code suggestions to files under the project root folder above.

Do not traverse outside (../) or into nested Xcode project bundles (.xcodeproj).

2. Existing Definitions

Before creating a new view, view model, service, or utility:

Search for relevant keywords in:

Views/

ViewModels/

Services/

Models/

Reuse or extend existing types if their responsibility overlaps.

Avoid duplicates: if a MapView, IncidentTypePicker, or Pin model already exists, update or compose rather than generate a new one.

3. File Organization Reference

App/: App entry points (AppDelegate.swift, DontpullupApp.swift)

Authentication/: Auth logic (AuthenticationManager.swift, AuthState.swift)

Models/: Data structures (Pin.swift, IncidentType.swift, User.swift)

ViewModels/: Business logic and state management

Services/: External integrations (Firebase, Networking, StorageUploader)

Views/: SwiftUI screens and components

Utilities/: Helpers and extensions

cursor/: Cursor.ai config (rules, MCP tools, scripts)

4. Naming & Patterns

Swift files: UpperCamelCase for types, lowerCamelCase for methods/properties

View files: End in View.swift (e.g., SettingsView.swift)

ViewModel files: End in ViewModel.swift

Service files: End in Manager.swift or Uploader.swift

Helper scripts: Place under cursor/ with .py or .sh extensions

5. Generation Guidelines

When asked to scaffold a new feature or file:

Check for existing similar files using the above directory map.

Search for key protocol or function names in root before generating. E.g., if adding a new CameraView, search import AVKit or Video in Views/ first.

If found, propose updating the existing file instead of creating a duplicate.

Save this file under the project root as cursor_context.md and include it in every request to Cursor.ai to provide accurate context and avoid duplication.

